# RISC0 Type and Import Standards

## Context
- Applied when working with RISC0 proof-related types and modules
- Focuses on correct import patterns for proof verification and seal data
- Ensures consistent usage of RISC0 v1.2.1 types
- See 2001-risc0-api for high-level RISC0 integration guidelines

## Requirements
- Import proof-related types from their correct crates and modules:
  - Use `risc0_groth16::{ProofJson, Seal}` for proof verification types
  - Use `risc0_zkp::core::digest::Digest as ProofDigest` for proof digests
  - Use `risc0_zkvm::Receipt` for receipt handling
  - Use `risc0_zkvm::sha::{Digest, Digestible}` when working with digests or MaybePruned types
  - NEVER import `Digest` directly from `risc0_zkvm` root
- Use correct module paths for digest types:
  - Import zkVM digests from `risc0_zkvm::sha::Digest` ONLY when needed
  - Import proof digests from `risc0_zkp::core::digest::Digest`
  - Import guest digests from `risc0_zkvm::guest::sha` when needed
  - Import `Digestible` trait when working with `MaybePruned` types
- Use appropriate type aliases to avoid ambiguity:
  - Alias `risc0_zkvm::sha::Digest as ReceiptDigest` ONLY when using both digest types
  - Alias `risc0_zkp::core::digest::Digest as ProofDigest` when using proof digests
  - Remove unused digest imports and aliases
- Never import from incorrect locations:
  - Avoid `bonsol_prover::proof::{ProofJson, Seal}`
  - Avoid `risc0_zkvm::Digest` (use `risc0_zkvm::sha::Digest` instead)
  - Avoid bare imports that could cause ambiguity
  - Remove any unused imports to prevent warnings

## Examples

<example>
// Good: Minimal required imports for proof handling with MaybePruned support
use risc0_groth16::{ProofJson, Seal};
use risc0_zkp::core::digest::Digest as ProofDigest;
use risc0_zkvm::{Receipt, InnerReceipt, sha::Digestible};

fn handle_proof(receipt: Receipt) -> Result<ProofDigest> {
    let claim = receipt.claim()?;
    let digest = claim.digest(); // Using Digestible trait on MaybePruned
    // Implementation
}
</example>

<example>
// Good: Imports when both digest types are needed
use risc0_zkp::core::digest::Digest as ProofDigest;
use risc0_zkvm::sha::{Digest as ReceiptDigest, Digestible};

fn process_proof(
    receipt: Receipt,
    proof_digest: ProofDigest,
    receipt_digest: ReceiptDigest,
) -> Result<()> {
    let claim = receipt.claim()?;
    let digest = claim.digest(); // Using Digestible trait
    // Implementation using both digest types
}
</example>

<example type="invalid">
// Bad: Missing Digestible trait for MaybePruned
use risc0_zkvm::Receipt;

fn handle_receipt(receipt: Receipt) -> Result<()> {
    let digest = receipt.claim()?.digest(); // Error: no method named 'digest' found for MaybePruned
    // Implementation
}
</example>

<example>
// Good: Direct digest usage with proper imports
use risc0_zkvm::sha::{Digest, Digestible};

fn hash_data(data: &[u8]) -> Digest {
    let mut digest = Digest::new();
    digest.update(data);
    digest
}
</example>

<example type="invalid">
// Bad: Ambiguous and incorrect imports
use risc0_zkvm::Digest;  // Wrong: must import from sha module
use risc0_zkp::core::digest::Digest;  // Conflicting import
use risc0_zkvm::sha::Digest as ReceiptDigest;  // Unnecessary if not using both types

fn process_data(data: Digest) {  // Ambiguous which Digest is used
    // Implementation
}
</example>

## Critical Notes
<critical>
- ALWAYS import Digestible trait when working with MaybePruned types
- NEVER use bare Digest imports without proper module path
- Use type aliases consistently to avoid ambiguity
- Remove unused imports to prevent confusion
</critical> 